<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Canal du Midi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1666–1681</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Canal du Midi is a canal in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> at Sète, which together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a> connects the Atlantic and the Mediterranean. Designed by Pierre-Paul Riquet and backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-colbert">Colbert</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, it was built between 1666 and 1681 and was one of the great engineering works of its age. It became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1996.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/canal-du-midi.jpg" alt="Canal du Midi" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tournasol7 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pont-canal_de_l%27Orb004.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
